{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of volatility: The model consistently forecasts a smooth, monotonic trend while true outcomes exhibit high-amplitude oscillations (spikes and sharp drops). Apply a volatility multiplier to the forecast range rather than a point estimate.",
    "Implement aggressive mean reversion after upward spikes: When history shows a rapid price increase (e.g., rising from 77 to 88), the model fails to anticipate the subsequent correction. Reduce forecasts by 5-10% immediately following any 3-day upward trend exceeding 5%.",
    "Adjust for sentiment lag: Positive sentiment signals (values > 0.20) in the history often precede short-term price drops or stagnation rather than sustained growth. Do not extrapolate positive sentiment linearly; treat it as a potential short-term top signal.",
    "Horizon-specific bias correction: The model exhibits a persistent upward bias in the first 5-10 days of the forecast horizon. Apply a negative bias correction of approximately 1.5-2.0 EUR to the first week of predictions.",
    "Invert trend extrapolation during consolidation: When the 18-day history shows a 'V' shape recovery (e.g., dropping to 77 then rising to 85), the model assumes a continued slow climb. Instead, anticipate a plateau or reversal in the forecast window."
  ]
}
